  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Def12 permits any universal prefix, including an empty or partial one, rather than requiring a prefix that binds every free variable. For example, if P(x) is open, Def13 counts both K P(x) → P(x) and K(K P(x) → P(x)) as axioms. Reinhardt specifies the universal closure and, optionally, K of that closed sentence. This is a difference in axiom membership, regardless of whether the open instances are derivable."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def14 allows T to contain open formulas and permits their direct use as hypotheses. Reinhardt defines deduction from T for sets of sentences. This extends the domain of the deduction relation; the hypothesis clause itself agrees when T consists of sentences."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "As acknowledged in the claim, the variable-only relational language limits instantiation to variables and does not express the source's arithmetic axioms, extended induction scheme, or Leibniz law for equality."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Within the declared fragment, the four special axiom schemas have the correct directions, and modus ponens is the sole inference rule. The material mismatch is that the optional outer K applies even to open axiom instances, not just to universal closures."
}
